<h2>What hydro jetting does</h2>
<p>A jetter pushes water through a hose to a nozzle with jets aimed backward and sometimes forward. The rear jets pull the hose up the pipe while scouring the wall, and the forward jets break up whatever is in the way. Different nozzles do different jobs: some rotate to scrub the full circumference, some are built to chew through roots, and some are designed to flush sand and debris toward the main.</p>
<p>For heavier work we combine jetting with mechanical tools, such as cable-driven cutters that slice through root masses or remove rust scale from cast iron. The goal is the same either way: a pipe interior the camera can see clearly, all the way down.</p>

<h2>Drain cleaning versus jetting</h2>
<p>A basic drain cable punches a hole through a clog so water can move again. That is often the right first response in an emergency, and we do it. But a cable leaves most of the blockage stuck to the pipe wall, and roots in particular come back quickly after they have been trimmed.</p>
<p>Jetting cleans the wall, not just a path through the middle. When roots are the cause, it removes much more of the growth, and it clears the grease and sludge that roots trap. It also leaves the line clean enough for a camera to tell us whether there is damage underneath.</p>

<h2>What we typically clear on the Front Range</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Roots at clay tile joints.</strong> Postwar subdivisions across the metro were built with short clay sections, and every joint is an invitation. Silver maple, cottonwood and Siberian elm are aggressive in older yards and along streets.</li>
  <li><strong>Scale in cast iron.</strong> Older Denver homes with cast iron drains build up rough, rusty scale that narrows the pipe and snags paper.</li>
  <li><strong>Grease.</strong> From kitchens at home and, in larger volume, from restaurants and commercial kitchens.</li>
  <li><strong>Sediment and debris.</strong> Soil that washed in through a cracked or separated joint, a sign of a pipe that needs more than cleaning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When cleaning is the whole answer</h2>
<p>If the camera after jetting shows a sound pipe with only light root entry at a joint or two, cleaning alone may buy you years. Some owners choose scheduled maintenance jetting instead of an immediate repair, and for a line in fair condition that can be a sensible choice. We will tell you if that is where you stand.</p>
<p>Where roots return every year or two to the same joints, the joints are the problem. Lining with <a href="/cipp-pipe-lining/">CIPP</a> seals them, and the maintenance cycle stops.</p>

<h2>Cleaning before lining is not optional</h2>
<p>A cured liner seats against whatever is on the pipe wall when it goes in. If that is a root mat, grease or loose scale, the liner is bonded to material that is already failing. So on every lining job we clean until the camera shows bare pipe, then run the camera again. That second look sometimes reveals damage the debris was hiding, and occasionally it changes the plan to <a href="/pipe-bursting/">pipe bursting</a> or a <a href="/sewer-line-replacement/">spot repair</a>. It is far better to learn that before the liner is ordered.</p>

<h2>When we do not jet</h2>
<p>Water under high pressure is aggressive. On pipe that is already cracked through, badly deformed Orangeburg, or cast iron worn thin along the bottom, careless jetting can open up what it is cleaning. That is why we look with the camera first whenever we can, choose pressure and nozzles to match the pipe, and switch to gentler tools when the line calls for it. If the pipe is too fragile to clean, we say so and talk about replacing it.</p>

<h2>Winter backups</h2>
<p>Colorado sewers are buried below frost depth, and a properly installed line should not freeze. When a backup appears in a cold snap, the cause is usually a sag holding water near the surface, a line that was installed shallow, or a blockage that let water stand in the pipe. We clear the line and camera it so you know which it was, because a line that has frozen once can split and will likely freeze again.</p>

<h2>Homes, businesses and the part under the street</h2>
<p>We jet residential laterals from the house to the main and larger lines at <a href="/commercial-trenchless/">commercial, multifamily and municipal properties</a>, including grease lines and building drains. In most Front Range cities and districts, the owner is responsible for the lateral all the way to the main, so if roots are entering near the tap, that section is yours to clean and repair too.</p>
